o=A.a(["Best Case: O(n\xb2) (Still forced to scan the entire remaining list to verify the minimum)","Average Case: O(n\xb2)","Worst Case: O(n\xb2)","Space Complexity: O(1)"],l)
q=A.ce(A.a7(["Python","def selection_sort(arr):\n    n = len(arr)\n    \n    for i in range(n):\n        # Assume the current boundary element is the minimum\n        min_idx = i\n        \n        # Scan the unsorted region to find the true minimum\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if arr[j] < arr[min_idx]:\n                min_idx = j\n                \n        # Swap the true minimum into its correct sorted position\n        arr[i], arr[min_idx] = arr[min_idx], arr[i]\n","Java","void selectionSort(int[] arr) {\n    int n = arr.length;\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n        // Assume the current boundary element is the minimum\n        int minIdx = i;\n        \n        // Scan the unsorted region to find the true minimum\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n            if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) {\n                minIdx = j;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Swap the true minimum into its correct sorted position\n        int temp = arr[minIdx];\n        arr[minIdx] = arr[i];\n        arr[i] = temp;\n    }\n}\n","C++","void selectionSort(vector<int>& arr) {\n    int n = arr.size();\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n        // Assume the current boundary element is the minimum\n        int minIdx = i;\n        \n        // Scan the unsorted region to find the true minimum\n        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n            if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) {\n                minIdx = j;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Swap the true minimum into its correct sorted position\n        swap(arr[minIdx], arr[i]);\n    }\n}\n","JavaScript","function selectionSort(arr) {\n    let n = arr.length;\n    \n    for (let i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {\n        // Assume the current boundary element is the minimum\n        let minIdx = i;\n        \n        // Scan the unsorted region to find the true minimum\n        for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {\n            if (arr[j] < arr[minIdx]) {\n                minIdx = j;\n            }\n        }\n        \n        // Swap the true minimum into its correct sorted position\n        let temp = arr[minIdx];\n        arr[minIdx] = arr[i];\n        arr[i] = temp;\n    }\n}\n"],h,h),o,p,"Master the art of the systematic scan! Learn how Selection Sort divides your array into a 'sorted' and 'unsorted' frontier. We will explore how it hunts for the absolute smallest element in the wilderness and surgically swaps it into place, executing the absolute minimum number of memory writes possible.","algorithms-selection-sort",m,"Selection Sort",A.a(["The Swapping Trap: Beginners often mistakenly execute physical swaps inside the inner loop the moment they find a smaller number. You must wait until the inner loop completely finishes to swap only the *true* minimum once.","The Best-Case Illusion: Unlike Bubble or Insertion Sort, Selection Sort has no boolean flags to detect a sorted array. It will relentlessly execute O(n\xb2) calculations even if you feed it a perfectly sorted list.","Stability Loss: Swapping a minimum element from the far right to the far left can cause it to physically jump over duplicate numbers, completely ruining their original sequential order."],l),q,A.a([A.a0(1,"In Selection Sort, finding the minimum element requires checking every single number in the unsorted portion of the array. Even if the array is perfectly sorted, the algorithm doesn't 'know' that until it finishes scanning every remaining element.",A.a(["Because it relies on a recursive call stack that cannot be interrupted.","Because it has no mechanism to verify if the list is sorted; it blindly scans the entire remaining unsorted region to prove it has found the minimum element.","Because it always sorts the data backward first.","Because the memory swap operation takes O(n) time."],l),"Why does Selection Sort always execute in O(n\xb2) time, even if the array is already perfectly sorted?"),A.a0(2,"While its O(n\xb2) comparison time is awful, Selection Sort only makes a maximum of O(n) physical memory swaps. If your hardware heavily penalizes writing to disk/memory, Selection Sort minimizes that specific wear-and-tear.",A.a(["When the CPU has a high number of processing cores.","When random access memory (RAM) is extremely large.","When writing data to physical memory (swapping) is incredibly slow or expensive (like on EEPROM or Flash memory).","When the dataset contains massive strings instead of integers."],l),"In what highly specific hardware scenario might Selection Sort actually be preferred over faster algorithms?"),A.a0(1,"Bubble sort is chaotic, constantly swapping adjacent elements as it steps through the array. Selection sort is methodical; it patiently looks at every element, finds the smallest one, and only makes a single swap at the very end of the pass.",A.a(["Selection sort uses recursion, while Bubble sort uses while loops.","Bubble sort repeatedly swaps adjacent elements to float the maximum value to the top, while Selection sort scans for the minimum value and executes exactly one targeted swap per pass.","Selection sort takes O(n) space, while Bubble sort takes O(1) space.","Bubble sort works backward, while Selection sort works from the middle."],l),"What is the core logical difference between Selection Sort and Bubble Sort?"),A.a0(2,"The entire point of the inner loop is to strictly *find* the minimum index. If you swap every time you see a smaller number, you are doing unnecessary physical memory writes, defeating the one major advantage of Selection Sort.",A.a(["The algorithm becomes O(1) Time Complexity.","The code crashes with an OutOfBounds exception.","The algorithm turns into a highly inefficient hybrid of Bubble Sort, executing excessive memory writes and ruining the O(n) swap advantage.","The array will be sorted in descending order instead of ascending."],l),"What happens if you accidentally place the element swap operation INSIDE the inner 'j' loop?")],g),"assets/mds/dsa/algorithms/selectionsort.md",B.a0O)
