b=A.ce(A.a7(["Python",'class Node:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef pre_order(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return\n        \n    # 1. Visit Root FIRST\n    print(node.val, end=" ")\n    \n    # 2. Traverse Left\n    pre_order(node.left)\n    \n    # 3. Traverse Right\n    pre_order(node.right)\n',"Java",'class Node {\n    int val;\n    Node left, right;\n    public Node(int item) { val = item; }\n}\n\nclass TreeTraversal {\n    public void preOrder(Node node) {\n        if (node == null) return;\n        \n        // 1. Visit Root FIRST\n        System.out.print(node.val + " ");\n        \n        // 2. Traverse Left\n        preOrder(node.left);\n        \n        // 3. Traverse Right\n        preOrder(node.right);\n    }\n}\n',"C++",'#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int val;\n    Node* left;\n    Node* right;\n    Node(int x) : val(x), left(NULL), right(NULL) {}\n};\n\nvoid preOrder(Node* node) {\n    if (node == NULL) return;\n    \n    // 1. Visit Root FIRST\n    cout << node->val << " ";\n    \n    // 2. Traverse Left\n    preOrder(node->left);\n    \n    // 3. Traverse Right\n    preOrder(node->right);\n}\n',"JavaScript","class Node {\n    constructor(val) {\n        this.val = val;\n        this.left = null;\n        this.right = null;\n    }\n}\n\nfunction preOrder(node) {\n    if (node === null) return;\n    \n    // 1. Visit Root FIRST\n    console.log(node.val);\n    \n    // 2. Traverse Left\n    preOrder(node.left);\n    \n    // 3. Traverse Right\n    preOrder(node.right);\n}\n"],a0,a0),q,a,"Pre-order Traversal is a Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithm that prioritizes the parent. The golden rule is: Root \u2794 Left \u2794 Right. It processes the current node immediately before diving as deeply as possible down the left side of the tree. Because it processes nodes from the top down, it is the absolute standard algorithm used to serialize, copy, or clone an entire tree structure.","tree-pre-order-traversal",m,l,A.a(["Confusing the DFS Orders: Mixing up the order of operations. Remember the name dictates where the ROOT is processed: Pre = Root First, In = Root Middle, Post = Root Last.","Ignoring Iterative Solutions: Relying solely on recursion. In production environments where tree depths are unpredictable, you should know how to write Pre-order using an explicit Stack to avoid memory crashes.","Missing the Base Case: Forgetting to check if the node is null before attempting to read its value or children, resulting in immediate Null Pointer Exceptions."],d),b,A.a([A.a0(1,"The prefix 'Pre' means 'Before'. Therefore, the Root is processed BEFORE traversing its Left and Right children.",A.a([h,g,f,"Root \u2794 Right Subtree \u2794 Left Subtree"],d),"What is the exact processing sequence for a Pre-order Traversal?"),A.a0(2,"Because Pre-order processes the parent before its children, it allows you to easily create a new parent node and then recursively attach cloned children to it.",A.a(["Safely deleting a tree from memory.","Retrieving data from a BST in numerical order.","Creating an exact copy or clone of a tree.","Finding the shortest path between two nodes."],d),"Which real-world scenario is Pre-order traversal best suited for?"),A.a0(2,"Depth-First Search (DFS) algorithms naturally use the system's Call Stack via recursion. To do it iteratively, you must manually simulate that behavior using your own Stack data structure.",A.a(["Queue","Hash Map","Stack","Linked List"],d),"If you convert a recursive Pre-order algorithm into an iterative one, which data structure MUST you use?"),A.a0(2,"To traverse a tree completely, you must visit every single node exactly once. Therefore, the time complexity scales linearly, making it O(N).",A.a(["O(1)","O(log N)","O(N)","O(N\xb2)"],d),"What is the time complexity of running Pre-order traversal on a tree with N nodes?")],a1),"assets/mds/dsa/trees/preorder.md",B.lA)
a=A.a(["The Sorting Magic: When performed on a Binary Search Tree (BST), In-order traversal magically visits every single node in perfectly sorted (ascending) order.","Infix Notation: When applied to an Expression Tree, it outputs standard math notation (e.g., A + B), exactly as humans read and write equations.","Flattening Trees: It is the go-to algorithm for flattening a BST into a sorted Array or converting it into a sorted Doubly Linked List in-place."],d)
q=A.a(["Terrible for Cloning: If you read a BST using In-order and immediately insert those values into a new tree, you will create a degenerate O(n) straight line (since the data is already sorted).","Unsafe for Deletion: You process the Root before its Right child. If you delete the Root, you lose the pointer to the rest of the tree!","Recursion Limits: Like all DFS approaches, it relies heavily on the Call Stack, meaning an unbalanced tree of 100,000 nodes will crash your program with a Stack Overflow."],d)
